A||||6||REDHOTT88||Decklid question: Torsion rod removal||||5||
Z||000000||REDHOTT88||12-08-2001||03:16 PM|||| I recently had my left decklid hinge break, and am going to replace one side only, without, hopefully, removing the decklid.<P>My question is... if I was to buy Mr. Rodney Dickmans strut kit, would I be able to just remove the torsion rod completely and not have one there at all? (But still have just the hinges)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">  Do I have to remove both? (More than likely right?)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> <P>The reason I ask is, my decklid wont close right at all now. The side next to hinge, near the back window keeps popping up. I'm afraid it might ruin something.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>So I thought I might be able to remove the torsion rod to keep it from poping up.<P>I'd like to repair so I can drive, but haven't located a hinge yet...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Sorry so long winded!!!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||88-DOHC||12-08-2001||03:30 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B> I recently had my left decklid hinge break, and am going to replace one side only, without, hopefully, removing the decklid.<P>My question is... if I was to buy Mr. Rodney Dickmans strut kit, would I be able to just remove the torsion rod completely and not have one there at all? (But still have just the hinges)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">    Do I have to remove both? (More than likely right?)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">   <P>The reason I ask is, my decklid wont close right at all now. The side next to hinge, near the back window keeps popping up. I'm afraid it might ruin something.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>So I thought I might be able to remove the torsion rod to keep it from poping up.<P>I'd like to repair so I can drive, but haven't located a hinge yet...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Sorry so long winded!!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Basicly yes to all your questions.  Although you will have to remove the decklib in order to remove the torsion rods. Just mark where the hinges attach to it so you can put it back where it was.  If you use Rodney's kit, just remove the torsion rods all together (both of them).  The strut kit Rodney has put together was well thought out and works great. Takes about 10 minutes to install (of course it takes longer to remove the torsion rods). Highly recommended for anyone wanting to use struts instead of the torsion rods.<P>You could just do the work removing the torsion rods first, that way your decklid will close and not pop up like you stated.  In fact, if you are having trouble finding a replacement hinge, I would say just go ahead and do this anyway. You don't need the torsion rods, just will have to have a stick or something to hold up the decklid in the mean time.  At least then the desklid will close and stay closed.<p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||12-08-2001||03:34 PM||||Mechanics tip: PUT A CHUNK OF PLYWOOD IN THE WINDOW SILL IN CASE THE THING GETS AWAY FROM YOU!!!!!!!!||||reg||12
Z||000003||Rodney||12-08-2001||04:19 PM||||Take a rope and tie the spring so it can't open farther and take the hinge out. Drill/punch out the broken pin and find a piece of steel that size and put it in the hinge. Should work and be easy to do. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||REDHOTT88||12-08-2001||04:19 PM||||AAAAHHHH.... I feel much better!<P>Thanks  for the help.<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">  P.S. Do you HAVE to remove decklid to take out rods? Can't I just unscrew center  and remove with hinges intact?<p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000005||88-DOHC||12-08-2001||04:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>AAAAHHHH.... I feel much better!<P>Thanks  for the help.<P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">   P.S. Do you HAVE to remove decklid to take out rods? Can't I just unscrew center  and remove with hinges intact?<P>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-08-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The little cable that ties the top part of the hinge to the lower that actually keeps the hinge from opening completely makes it so you can't do what you described.  Its far easier doing the work necassary to remove the torsion rods with the decklid off that its worth the 5 minutes it takes to remove said decklid. That is, if you really want to remove them and replace them with struts.  <P>Rodney's comment above is a good way to solve the problem without replacing the whole hinge. I have never had a broken hinge, nor really looked at what they use as the hinge point, so I can't say how hard or easy it would be to do this. Can't imagine it being too difficult though, just as long as you keep the torsion rod from doing its own thing (hence the reason you would tie it down).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||REDHOTT88||12-08-2001||04:39 PM||||You guys are great!!<P>Thanks a lot!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
